Z||000000||RobertVol||06-03-2001||12:23 AM||||I am a pretty good computer person in most cases, do my own web pages and work with computers all day at work, but I am totally stumped.<P>I have a small processor 500mhz (which doesnt mean anything) and I have 192mbs RAM.<P>Now my question is:<P>I have only have my ISP program up with my Web publishing Software. My resources show 90% then as I continue to work on my computer the resources must go down because I lose my connection to my ISP and then as soon as that happens I check my resources and they have dropped to 75%. What gives?<P>I have seen this at work with IP's I am logged into and then they have a system memory leak and it continues to eat my RAM until I either close the connection with my IP I am connected to or my other programs drop and I reboot. But this is at home.<P>Any suggestions why the resources continue to drop? <P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club<P><A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/RobsGT2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||RobertVol||06-03-2001||02:14 AM||||Hmm.<P>Do I have everyone stumped? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroSTETZ||06-03-2001||04:05 AM||||go to <A HREF="http://www.download.com" TARGET=_blank>www.download.com</A>  and get dumeter, it will give insight to your bandwidth, also, ctrl alt del to see whats running in the background - if you have a static ip, someone might be camping that ip waiting for you to sign on, so maybe even a firewall would not be a bad idea. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||JSocha||06-03-2001||04:24 AM||||What operating system and what is the latest service patch level you are running on it?  How about the programs you are running?<P>I just got done running Norton Utilities 2000 (since I got hit by a Virus at work on Thursday) here on my home computer before checking EMAIL so I didn't get the bug.<P>Went through all the utilities and now my system crashes less, I get less ERRORS or non-responding messages, little bit more zippy and my programs are more stable.  Now if I can just isolate why my computer LOCKS up all of a sudden when I try to EMAIL out or upload an image or visit many web sites, I think I will have a stable computer once again.<P>What programs are you typically running in the background?  What programs are being loaded into your system tray?  <P>You may want to try to go into MSCONFIG and shut all TSR's down, remove any programs from your STARTUP folder and gradually put them back on one by one, test your system for a couple of days, do it again until you start with your STARTUP folder until you find out which program suddenly starts hogging your resources.  It will take awhile, but the shotgun approach typically works by isolating it.<P>When your resources start running low again when a particular program is turned back on, leave it on and shut the others off again to see if that is your culprit.<P>Your system may also just need a good tweek and/or tune up to remove any programs that are no longer used, have been deleted and/or still have registry entries that are no longer valid!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||4
Z||000004||Tweek||06-03-2001||10:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JSocha:<BR><B><BR>Your system may also just need a good tweek....<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Someone calls?||||reg||1
